{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794480702,"nonce":"IFDIfrn7koTkHlM8+vktelElWaU=","message":"Where the remaining build time goes, and a warm rebuild, 2026-11-12. NUC, same binary and inputs as the previous comment, pinned to cores 0,2,4,6, load about 2, default direct build with fibers. Single runs, not medians.\n\nCorrection to the previous table: those NUC builds wrote their output away from the four library modules, so ash did not export the 97 runtime functions the libraries import. With the libraries beside the output (the real layout, and the one the Mac runs used): default with fibers 3.37 s wall (15.11 MB), LLVM with fibers 18.5 s wall, 31.4 cpu-s, 1797 MB (2 builds, 17.89 MB). That is 5.5x, not 5.2x.\n\nStage times from --air-cache-stats (ms; direct_program is decode + AIR + emit of all 4083 functions, link is the wasm link including the fiber transform):\n\n                      cold, cache written   warm (4083 of 4083 from cache)\n    wall                    4.69 s               2.78 s\n    direct_program          3158                 1507\n    link                    1375                 1206\n      of which fibers        676                  740\n    AIR passes              1139                    0\n    cache write              426                    0\n    cache read                62                   38\n\nWithout a cache the same build is 3.37 s (no cache write). The warm case is the cache hitting every function for an unchanged program; it saves the AIR passes (about 1.1 s) and nothing else. A rebuild after a Haxe edit is not measured: it needs the Haxe compile, and the NUC has no ashui libraries.\n\nWhere the rest is (sampler on the Mac at load 176, so shares only, and it disagrees with the NUC stage times on the size of the fiber transform: about 45% of samples there against 25% here): the fiber transform (plan, add_rewind_dispatch, reading every function's operators and re-encoding it) and the emitter (do_tree, Liveness::analyze with its BTreeSet inserts) are the two big items; malloc and free are about 14% of self time. The transform re-reads and re-encodes the whole module, including the 8 MB runtime object, on every build.\n\nNot done: any change. The two places to look first are the fiber transform's read-and-reencode of runtime code that does not change between builds, and Liveness::analyze.","files":null}]}